      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please fix this WARNING reported by autoreview issue finding: If `next` is a `-\u003d` operation, `is_modification()` will return `true` (it\u0027s true for both `+\u003d` and `-\u003d`). This means a `-\u003d` would be incorrectly converted into `\u003d`, changing the semantics from removing items to assigning them. \n\nAdditionally, if `next` is `-\u003d`, we shouldn\u0027t remove the preceding `a \u003d []` either, because `-\u003d` requires the variable to be already initialized. \n\nConsider explicitly checking for `Token::MINUS_EQUALS` to avoid breaking `-\u003d` assignments:\n\n```cpp\n        } else if (next \u0026\u0026 !assign.is_conditional() \u0026\u0026\n                   (!next-\u003eis_conditional() || assignments.size() \u003d\u003d 2)) {\n          auto* next_op \u003d next-\u003enode()-\u003eAsBinaryOpMut();\n          if (next_op-\u003eop().type() \u003d\u003d Token::MINUS_EQUALS) {\n            // Cannot safely remove the initial `a \u003d []` if the next operation is `-\u003d`\n            // because `a -\u003d ...` requires `a` to be initialized, and we cannot convert\n            // `-\u003d` to `\u003d`.\n          } else {\n            if (next-\u003eis_modification() \u0026\u0026 !assign.is_modification()) {\n              next_op-\u003eset_op(Token(next_op-\u003eop().location(), Token::EQUAL, \"\u003d\"));\n            }\n            assign.RemoveSelfUnconditionally();\n          }\n        }\n```",
